//   account-model review. This template is the spine: load the program-under-test's `.so`, set
//   up the exact accounts/PDAs/mints an attacker would, send the ATTACKER instruction, and end
//   on a QUANTIFIED assertion — the same "focused test → assertion" shape as Foundry.
//
//   LiteSVM is in-process (no validator), so a PoC runs in milliseconds. Use it first. Escalate
//   to solana-program-test/BanksClient or `anchor test`/local-validator ONLY on a runtime-
//   semantics wall (see templates/solana/README.md), and record why in the ledger row.
//
// CONTRACT (fill per target)
//   Precondition : the program + honest actors deployed the SAME way an integration deploy would
//                  (correct program id — PDAs derive from it — mints, ATAs, initial state).
//   Postcondition: the named theft/lock is demonstrated on CONCRETE on-chain reads (attacker's
//                  token balance up / vault down / a legit user's funds now unwithdrawable) — NOT
//                  on a ghost counter this harness itself wrote.
//   Invariant    : the quantified property that MUST hold (Σ token balances conserved; only the
//                  owner-signed authority can move funds; shares priced from tracked reserves).
//                  The PoC breaks exactly one such invariant.
//   Canary       : assert the ATTACK STATE WAS REACHED (the attacker ix actually executed and
//                  landed the deep state) before trusting any "held / REFUTED" result. A green
//                  test that never drove the vulnerable instruction is a lie (repo rule).
//
// SETUP
//   1) Build the target: `anchor build` (Anchor) or `cargo build-sbf` → target/deploy/<prog>.so.
//      MATCH the target's Anchor/Solana pins first (avm use <ver>) — see docs/solana-notes.md §3.
